{"kind":"artifact","text":"task/deployment-story revised at exact head 2e1b23cf15212c0f453f8eb84149321ca4dce18c, addressing all four blocking findings. (1) Control-plane isolation: the tier-1 design handed the sequencer key to every submitter, because a push-triggered workflow runs the file as it exists on the pushed ref and repository secrets are in scope for workflows on any branch. The workflow and secret now live in a separate repository submitters cannot write, the drain runs on schedule and repository_dispatch rather than push, and a ruleset confines submitters to refs/heads/inbox/**; the single-repository arrangement is recorded as a weaker fallback. New spike case 7 is the malicious-workflow threat case and must pass before tier 1 is offered — a pass there is four refusals, not four green runs. (2) Ref identity: the name now carries genesis, namespace, fingerprint and the idempotency key in unpadded lowercase base32hex, and those four components are the deduplication identity, deliberately the same identity the log dedups on. I measured the encoding claim rather than recalling it: against git check-ref-format on 2.50.1, space, .., ~, ^, :, ?, *, [, backslash, leading and trailing /, trailing .lock, trailing ., @{, control characters and DEL are each refused raw and each legal once encoded. (3) Terminal records: the old protocol could lose accepted work two ways, so the sequence push is now the commit point, claims are taken by compare-and-swap rename so concurrent runs cannot double-drain, refusals are written to a retained note instead of an expiring ref, and a stale claim is re-drained rather than deleted. Nothing is removed on a timer. New spike case 8 kills a run on each side of the commit point; case 9 is the ref-name adversary. (4) Claims narrowed: git does not fetch refs/seq without attach, so the record is reachable to whoever knows to ask rather than discoverable by cloning; and signatures detect a forged history but do not recover a deleted one, so retention is a stated replication policy and one published copy is availability, not durability. R8 now says backup is clone plus attach, and R9 says a checkpoint held only by the host that wrote it bounds nothing. Gates green: vet, test, git diff --check, worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"2e1b23cf15212c0f453f8eb84149321ca4dce18c","path":"notes/2026-08-07-deployment.md"}}